 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Out of Memory (OOM)**: Processing all points for a batch simultaneously can exceed GPU memory [S4, unofficial]. Solution: Split query points into chunks (e.g., 4096 points) and process sequentially [S4, unofficial].
-*   **Precision**: Use float32 for standard training [G]. Mixed precision can be used but requires careful handling of loss scaling [G].
-*   **Degenerate Input**: If near/far planes are not set correctly relative to the scene scale, sampling may miss the object or include excessive empty space, leading to poor convergence [G].
-*   **Distortion Regularization**: Standard NeRF does not apply distortion regularization [S1], unlike mip-NeRF which uses it to improve anti-aliasing [3409-mip-nerf-optimization-and-activation-details.md].
+*   **Memory Overflow**: Large batches or high-resolution images can exceed GPU memory. Mitigated by processing points in chunks (`chunksize`) [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Precision**: Floating-point precision in transmittance calculation can lead to underflow/overflow. Use stable exponential implementations [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Degenerate Input**: Ensure camera poses and intrinsics are correctly formatted. Invalid near/far planes can cause sampling errors [S4, unofficial].
+*   **Overfitting**: Small datasets may require regularization or early stopping [S1].
 
